@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Workspace dependency trees — one probe, one table, every language

' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh, defs/opencode/entrypoint.sh,
'         defs/cursor/entrypoint.sh, defs/cecli/entrypoint.sh,
'         defs/claudecode/mcp/entrypoint.sh

node "Host checkout — bind-mounted at /app" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "portable majority\nJS · gems · .lua · source" as PORT <<db>>
  database "platform binaries\n""\.node"" · ""\.bundle"" · ""\.so"" · providers" as BIN <<error>>
}

node "Container — ensure_dependency_trees" as C <<host>> {
  component "**discover** — ""_proveo_project_roots""\nper-language markers" as SCAN <<app>>
  component "**classify** — ""_dep_lang_class""\naddons · artifacts · provisioned · portable" as CLASS <<app>>
  component "**probe** — ""_proveo_foreign_object""\nELF magic, capped scan" as PROBE <<app>>
  component "**name** — ""_dep_owner""\nper-ecosystem identity" as OWNER <<app>>
  component "**repair** — ""_dep_reinstall""\nopt-in only" as FIX <<async>>
}

cloud "Package registry" as REG <<cloud>>

PORT ARROW_MAIN C : loads fine — which is the trap
BIN ARROW_ERROR PROBE : cannot execute here
SCAN ARROW_MAIN CLASS
CLASS ARROW_MAIN PROBE : addons · artifacts
PROBE ARROW_MAIN OWNER : any non-ELF object
OWNER ARROW_OPTIONAL FIX : ""PROVEO_DEPS=reinstall"" · addons only
FIX ARROW_CLOUD REG : fetch
FIX ARROW_ERROR PORT : rewrites the operator's own tree
REG ARROW_ERROR FIX : off the allowlist on every tier but ""open""

note bottom of BIN
  **This is not a TypeScript hazard.** node_modules is the loudest instance, not
  the only one: ruby's ""vendor/bundle"" carries compiled gem extensions, luarocks
  ships ""\.so"" modules, and ""\.terraform"" holds provider EXECUTABLES pinned per
  ""os_arch"". Every one of them crosses the mount as a host binary.

  What differs per language is not WHETHER the tree crosses, but what it costs —
  so the remedy is a table, not a special case per ecosystem.
end note

note right of CLASS
  **Every supported language gets a row, including the ones with nothing to do** —
  an absent row is indistinguishable from an oversight.

  ""addons""      mostly-portable tree punctuated by platform binaries; the package
                manager rebuilds it, given a reachable registry.
                → typescript · ruby · lua · terraform
  ""artifacts""   entirely host build output; no registry needed, the toolchain
                regenerates it once the stale tree is out of the way.
                → rust · cpp · zig
  ""provisioned"" never inherited at all — a venv ALWAYS holds a platform
                interpreter, so ""ensure_python_env"" builds it outside the workspace.
                → python
  ""portable""    nothing host-specific lands in-tree: Go modules and ""vendor/"" are
                source, Java/Kotlin resolve to bytecode JARs, and Nix keeps its
                closure in ""/nix/store"", which is never mounted.
                → go · java · kotlin · nix
end note

note left of PROBE
  ELF is the only object format this container can execute, so four bytes settle
  it — no ""uname"", no package metadata, no per-ecosystem special cases.

  ""\.a"" and ""\.rlib"" are deliberately NOT probed: both are ar archives on every
  platform, so their magic cannot tell host from container. A format that cannot
  answer the question does not belong in the probe.

  Capped at ""PROVEO_DEPS_PROBE_LIMIT"" (60) — a pnpm store holds thousands of
  binaries and the answer never changes after the first foreign one. This is a
  diagnosis, not an audit.
end note

note bottom of REG
  **The reinstall is a trade, and ""--clone"" is the way out of it.** Rebuilding a
  foreign tree writes Linux binaries into the operator's own checkout, so their
  host builds break until they reinstall — which makes the tree foreign again and
  earns another reinstall on the next run. One each way, indefinitely.

  ""proveo run <target> --clone"" ends it: sbx builds the workspace by CLONING the
  host repo, so only TRACKED files cross. Measured — a Mach-O ""node_modules"" on
  the host, absent inside the clone, and still Mach-O on the host afterwards. The
  seed then installs Linux dependencies into the clone, and the checkout is never
  written at all. Changes come back with ""git fetch sandbox-<name>"".
end note

note bottom of FIX
  **Why the repair is opt-in, on two counts.** It writes into the mounted tree,
  which IS the operator's checkout — a sandbox that silently rewrites the host's
  dependencies has defeated its own purpose. And under any egress tier but
  ""open"" the registry is off the allowlist, so the install fails partway and
  leaves the tree PARTIAL: strictly worse than the host-built one it replaced,
  which at least carried everything portable.

  ""artifacts"" never reach here at all — no registry can help a stale ""target/"",
  and the only advice that does is to remove it.
end note
@enduml
